{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Who had not known a man.</b> It should have said 'asher lo yeda'an ish' (who had not known a man, in feminine), as it says, 'a virgin, and no man had known her.' Rather, it is a convention, for it is the way of the man to initiate relations with the woman; but Lot's daughters were promiscuous and it was their way to seduce, as indeed they seduced their father. Therefore it is written of them 'asher lo yadu ish' (who had not known a man). And so with the daughters of Midian, who were promiscuous and seduced Israel, it is written of them, 'every woman who knows a man.'" "<b>[Only to these men].</b> To those whom you called 'men.' For therefore — since they came under the shelter of my roof — so that they would have no disturbance."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The bracketed text appears to be a gloss; the second sentence is textually difficult and translated hyper-literally. The word טרדא (disturbance/trouble) and the logic of the argument are somewhat obscure."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:52.882526055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}